---
import OdeV2Layout from '../../../layouts/OdeV2Layout.astro';
import Section from '../../../components/ode/Section.astro';
import DataTag from '../../../components/ode-v2/DataTag.astro';
import Status from '../../../components/ode-v2/Status.astro';
import Finding from '../../../components/ode-v2/Finding.astro';
import Callout from '../../../components/ode-v2/Callout.astro';
import StatCard from '../../../components/ode-v2/StatCard.astro';
import ReadNext from '../../../components/ode-v2/ReadNext.astro';
import { odeMeta, odeRoutes } from '../../../i18n/ode-v2';

const lang = 'en' as const;
const m = odeMeta[lang].tesi;
const r = odeRoutes[lang];

const acid = [
  { m: 'Crude bleached calf tallow', v: '0.8', ffa: '0.4%', n: 'The raw material sits well on the parameter that the chemistry identifies as critical.' },
  { m: 'Pure rendered lard, reference limit', v: '1.3', ffa: '0.65%', n: 'The most stringent limit in the whole family of animal fats. It reflects raw material selection, not a property of the species.' },
  { m: 'Edible tallow, reference limit', v: '2.5', ffa: '1.25%', n: 'A food-grade commercial threshold, not a skin safety threshold. Nobody has ever tied it to a cutaneous outcome.' },
  { m: 'Chemically interesterified tallow, 9:1 blend', v: '17.4', ffa: '8.8%', n: 'Roughly seven times the reference limit. This is the kind of material on which the little existing human evidence rests.' },
];

const profile = [
  { fa: 'C16:0 palmitic', a: '29.88%', b: '30.3%' },
  { fa: 'C18:0 stearic', a: '12.00%', b: '16.2%' },
  { fa: 'C18:1 oleic', a: '45.19%', b: '35.4%' },
  { fa: 'C14:0 myristic', a: '5.76%', b: '6.3%' },
  { fa: 'C16:1 palmitoleic', a: '5.38%', b: '3.2%' },
  { fa: 'C18:2 linoleic', a: '0.50%', b: '1.3%' },
];

const lipase = [
  { mat: 'Beef tallow, pork fat', cls: 'Triglycerides', bond: 'Yes, three ester bonds per molecule', out: 'Exposed' },
  { mat: 'Vegetable oils (coconut, olive, sunflower)', cls: 'Triglycerides', bond: 'Yes', out: 'Exposed as much as tallow' },
  { mat: 'Petrolatum', cls: 'Mixture of saturated hydrocarbons', bond: 'No', out: 'Immune' },
  { mat: 'Mineral oil', cls: 'Liquid hydrocarbons', bond: 'No', out: 'Immune' },
  { mat: 'Squalane', cls: 'Hydrocarbon', bond: 'No', out: 'Immune' },
  { mat: 'Dimethicone and silicones', cls: 'Siloxane polymer', bond: 'No', out: 'Immune' },
];

const cannot = [
  {
    t: 'Occlusion, against petrolatum',
    d: 'No triglyceride comes close to petrolatum as an occlusive. We do not claim occlusive superiority, and we will not do so even if the experiment goes our way: the best possible favourable outcome is non-inferiority to mineral oil.',
  },
  {
    t: 'Barrier repair, against ceramide systems',
    d: 'In a randomised trial on 121 patients, severity scores for a physiological lipid mixture in the correct ratio do not differ significantly from those of a mid-potency corticosteroid at 28 days, which is not the same as demonstrating equivalence. Tallow supplies neither ceramides nor cholesterol in physiological ratio. We do not claim barrier repair in the physiological sense.',
  },
  {
    t: 'Stickiness, on the primary endpoint of our own category',
    d: 'Tallow belongs to the emollient category, whose stated purpose is to restore softness, flexibility and lubricity to the surface. Stickiness produces the opposite friction profile. It was measured instrumentally, with a texture analyser, on beef tallow: that is, by instrument and on our primary matrix, not by panel judgment on a different one. It is a failure on the category’s main endpoint, not a detail of acceptability.',
  },
  {
    t: 'Linoleic acid content',
    d: 'Tallow is poor in linoleic acid, between 0.5% and 1.3% across two independent characterisations. The cosmetic literature on lipids attributes barrier function largely to oils rich in linoleic and alpha-linolenic acid. This is an unfavourable datum and it has no mitigating clause.',
  },
];
---
<OdeV2Layout title={m.title} description={m.description} lang={lang} active="tesi">
  <header class="pg">
    <p class="ode-eyebrow">The Thesis</p>
    <h1>One parameter decides, and almost nobody declares it</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      This page sets out the chemistry that supports OdE, the complication that erodes it, and
      why both lead to the conclusion that the product is the qualification rather than the
      tallow. It contains our strongest argument and our most serious vulnerability, in the
      order a competent technical reader would meet them.
    </p>
  </header>

  <Section n="1" title="The principle is about one molecule, not a class">
    <p>
      A fatty acid locked into a triglyceride behaves like a brick in a wall.
      <strong>Oleic acid</strong> unbound behaves like a loose brick: it slides between the
      ordered layers of the skin's own barrier and disturbs them.
    </p>
    <p>
      This has been measured directly. In a study on the human forearm, applying the substances
      neat and with no penetration-enhancing vehicle, free oleic acid and its blends with more
      than 25% free form significantly raised transepidermal water loss and tracer penetration at
      24 hours, while the corresponding triglyceride at 100% produced no statistically detectable
      change.
      <DataTag kind="fonte" label="Mack Correa MC, Mao G, Saad P, Flach CR, Mendelsohn R, Walters RM. Molecular interactions of plant oil components with stratum corneum lipids. Exp Dermatol 2014;23(1):39-44. DOI 10.1111/exd.12296. Human volar forearm in vivo, neat substances, no propylene glycol." />
    </p>
    <p class="ev-line"><Status kind="dimostrato" lang={lang} /> <Finding kind="pro" lang={lang} /></p>

    <Callout tone="warn" title="A correction that concerns us, and that runs opposite to what we expected">
      <p>
        Until the most recent revision of our research this principle was stated in general
        form: "a free fatty acid disturbs the barrier, the esterified form does not."
        <strong>It was wrong through lack of precision.</strong> The study tests oleic acid
        against its triglyceride: the result concerns oleic acid, not the class of free fatty
        acids, and it does not extend to the saturated ones.
      </p>
      <p>
        The distinction matters because the two dominant saturated fatty acids in tallow,
        palmitic and stearic, together make up roughly 46% of the profile, and for them the
        evidence points the other way: applied topically to human skin ex vivo they increase
        lipid production and transport in the stratum corneum.
        <DataTag kind="fonte" label="Nip J et al., Unilever R&amp;D. International Journal of Cosmetic Science 2024. DOI 10.1111/ics.12961. Human skin ex vivo, tape-stripped, isolated fatty acids rather than tallow. Grade B1, evidence translated from damaged skin to the maintenance of healthy skin; authors with a declarable commercial interest." />
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>What this does NOT license us to say.</strong> Not that tallow strengthens or
        repairs the barrier: the study is on damaged skin, ex vivo, with isolated fatty acids
        rather than with tallow, and its authors have a commercial interest. The correct use is
        narrow and singular: <strong>it removes the assumption that everything released by
        hydrolysis is harmful</strong>. That is the premise of section 3, and nothing more.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <p>
      In tallow the fatty acid is <strong>bound</strong>. The question that matters, therefore,
      is not whether tallow contains oleic acid, but <strong>how much of it has come
      loose</strong>. That number has a name, a standard and a price: it is the acid value.
    </p>

    <Callout tone="honest" title="The two qualifications a technical reader will check first">
      <p>
        <strong>Transferability is not demonstrated.</strong> Almost all the barrier evidence
        uses liquid unsaturated vegetable oils, not saturated semisolid animal fats. The
        principle is mechanistically grounded and consistent with the corpus, but nobody has
        replicated that comparison on a saturated animal fat.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>No acid value threshold has been validated against a cutaneous outcome.</strong>
        Not for tallow and not for any other topical lipid. The limits cited below are commercial
        grade thresholds, built for a different purpose. Defining the relationship between acid
        value and barrier outcome is precisely the research space we claim, and it is
        <a href={r.evidenza}>still to be walked</a>.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </Section>

  <Section n="2" title="Acid value, and what it costs to know it">
    <p>
      Acid value measures how many fatty acids have detached from the triglyceride, in
      milligrams of potassium hydroxide per gram of fat. It is a standardised parameter, the
      determination is a titration, and on the public price list of an accredited laboratory it
      costs a few tens of euros.
      <DataTag kind="fonte" label="Acid value and free fatty acids according to EN ISO 660. Indicative price of roughly 35-60 euros per sample on the public price list of an analytical laboratory, verified in research track 6 v2 section 8 and in the OdE research consolidation, part D.3." />
    </p>
    <p class="measure">
      It is measurable, it is cheap, and it is the parameter on which safety depends according to
      the chemistry in section 1. And almost nobody in this market declares it.
    </p>

    <div class="tbl-wrap">
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Material or limit</th><th scope="col">mg KOH/g</th><th scope="col">FFA</th><th scope="col">What it means</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {acid.map((a) => (
            <tr><th scope="row">{a.m}</th><td class="num">{a.v}</td><td class="num">{a.ffa}</td><td>{a.n}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      <DataTag kind="fonte" label="Values measured on crude calf tallow and on interesterified blends: Kowalska M, Woźniak M, Żbikowska A, Kozłowska M. Biomolecules 2020;10:115. DOI 10.3390/biom10010115, determination per ISO 660, mean of three determinations." />
      <DataTag kind="fonte" label="Acid value and peroxide value limits for pure rendered lard, rendered pork fat, premier jus and edible tallow: FAO/WHO Codex Alimentarius, Standard for Named Animal Fats CXS 211-1999, Section 3 and Appendix, primary text." />
    </p>

    <h3 class="sub-h">The process generates acidity, and the process is controllable</h3>
    <p class="measure">
      Comparing rendering methods on the same raw material shows that process conditions
      determine the result. Against high-temperature rendering, low-temperature rendering yields
      an acid value roughly 3.8 times lower and peroxides roughly 2.3 times lower, with
      statistically significant differences.
      <DataTag kind="fonte" label="Limmatvapirat C, Limmatvapirat S, Krongrawa W, et al. Beef tallow: extraction, physicochemical property, fatty acid composition, antioxidant activity, and formulation of lotion bars. J Appl Pharm Sci 2021;11(09):018-028. DOI 10.7324/JAPS.2021.110903. Acid value 0.20-0.22 with rendering at 80 °C for 10 minutes, against 0.73-0.76 with microwave rendering at 135 °C for 5 minutes." />
    </p>
    <p class="measure">
      The same study must also be read honestly where it does not suit us: the differences in
      antioxidant activity between methods are <strong>not</strong> statistically significant.
      The rendering method affects acidity and peroxides, not antioxidant activity.
    </p>
    <Callout tone="honest" title="And a tension with no single answer">
      <p>
        The same work shows that the softer fraction of tallow has more unsaturates and higher
        measured antioxidant activity. But more unsaturates also means
        <strong>greater susceptibility to oxidation over time</strong> and a
        <strong>lower solid fat content at skin temperature</strong>, that is, less of the
        physical class we discuss in section 4.
      </p>
      <p>
        The fraction that optimises in vitro antioxidant activity is not the one that optimises
        shelf stability, and not the one that optimises persistence on skin.
        <strong>Fractionation has no single optimum: it has a trade-off curve, and that curve
        has to be mapped rather than asserted.</strong> It is one of the experiments listed in
        <a href={r.evidenza}>Open evidence</a>, and among the most expensive.
      </p>
    </Callout>
    <p class="ev-line"><Status kind="dimostrato" lang={lang} /> <Finding kind="pro" lang={lang} /></p>

    <Callout tone="warn" title="A detail that reverses how the existing evidence reads">
      <p>
        The only studies with a human outcome on tallow use chemically interesterified material,
        with an acid value up to roughly seven times the reference limit. That is precisely the
        kind of material the chemistry in section 1 flags as potentially disruptive.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is not an argument in our favour. It is an argument that
        <strong>the literature describes a different material from ours</strong>: ovine,
        interesterified, blended and high in acidity. Which means we cannot use it either for or
        against.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <h3 class="sub-h">The profile, for completeness</h3>
    <div class="tbl-wrap">
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Fatty acid</th><th scope="col">Characterisation A</th><th scope="col">Characterisation B</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {profile.map((p) => (
            <tr class:list={[p.fa.includes('linoleic') && 'is-gap']}>
              <th scope="row">{p.fa}</th><td class="num">{p.a}</td><td class="num">{p.b}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      Two independent gas chromatography characterisations with flame ionisation detection, on
      beef tallow.
      <DataTag kind="fonte" label="Characterisation A: Limmatvapirat 2021, DOI 10.7324/JAPS.2021.110903, low-temperature sample. Characterisation B: Kowalska 2020, DOI 10.3390/biom10010115, crude calf tallow. Highlighted row: linoleic acid content, an unfavourable datum." />
      The dashed row is the one that is unfavourable to us, and it is dashed on purpose.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section n="3" title="The complication, stated where the claim is expected">
    <p>
      The bacteria living on skin secrete lipases, enzymes whose job is to cut exactly that bond.
      They can free the fatty acid <strong>where the product is sitting</strong>, after it has
      left our control. Tallow with a low acid value applied to skin does not necessarily stay
      low in acid value.
    </p>
    <p class="ev-line"><Status kind="dimostrato" lang={lang} /> <Finding kind="contro" lang={lang} /></p>
    <p class="measure">
      The mechanism is certain. <strong>The direction of its effect is not</strong>, and this is
      a recent revision of our research that has to be stated in full, because it changes the
      nature of the problem. What lipases release is not one substance: it is a
      <strong>mixture</strong>, roughly 38% oleic acid, which section 1 identifies as
      unfavourable, and roughly 46% palmitic and stearic, for which the available evidence runs
      the other way. <strong>The net effect has never been measured.</strong>
    </p>
    <p class="measure">
      This is not good news in disguise: it is the move from a conflict between sources to a
      defined experimental gap, which is a better position only because it is solvable. The
      operational consequence is precise, and it is in
      <a href={r.evidenza}>Open evidence</a>: the experiment must not measure how fast the
      material hydrolyses, it must measure <strong>what</strong> it releases.
    </p>
    <p class="measure">
      The chain holds in every documentable link: skin lipases are characterised, the
      triglycerides of the fatty acids present in tallow are their substrates, and an intact
      triglyceride offered to a skin bacterium is cleaved with release of the corresponding fatty
      acid.
      <DataTag kind="fonte" label="Ingham E, Holland KT, Gowland G, Cunliffe WJ. Partial purification and characterization of lipase from Propionibacterium acnes. J Gen Microbiol 1981;124:393-401. DOI 10.1099/00221287-124-2-393." />
      <DataTag kind="fonte" label="Bomar L, Brugger SD, Yost BH, Davies SS, Lemon KP. Corynebacterium accolens releases antipneumococcal free fatty acids from human nostril and skin surface triacylglycerols. mBio 2016;7:e01725-15. DOI 10.1128/mBio.01725-15. Documented hydrolysis of intact triolein." />
    </p>

    <h3 class="sub-h">How large the effect is, with the data we have</h3>
    <p class="measure">
      The figure that circulates, roughly 90% hydrolysis, is measured <strong>inside the
      comedo</strong>: an enclosed environment, high bacterial density, low oxygenation, long
      residence. The same study, on the same population, measures roughly 25% on the
      <strong>surface of intact skin</strong>. And the bacterial contribution has a measured
      ceiling: eliminating 99% of the bacteria reduces surface free fatty acids by only 50%.
    </p>
    <div class="grid">
      <StatCard value="about 25%" caption="Hydrolysed fraction on the surface of intact skin, in the same study that measures roughly 90% inside the comedo." kind="fonte" source="Nicolaides N, Wells GC, et al. Lipid composition of comedones compared with that of human skin surface. J Invest Dermatol 1970;54:487-495. PMID 5454099." />
      <StatCard value="at most half" caption="Share of the surface free fatty acid load attributable to bacteria: eliminating 99% of them reduces it by only 50%." kind="fonte" source="Marples RR, Downing DT, Kligman AM. Control of free fatty acids in human surface lipids by Corynebacterium acnes. J Invest Dermatol 1971;56:127-131. PMID 4997367." />
      <StatCard value="not measured" caption="The composition of the mixture released from OdE material, which is the datum that decides the sign of the effect. No study has measured which fatty acids appear, and in what proportion, after applying an intact triglyceride to human skin." kind="bench" />
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      <Status kind="costruzione" lang={lang} />
      Inferring from these two elements that the expected order of magnitude on intact skin is
      closer to 25% than to 90% is an <strong>inference from anatomical context</strong>, not a
      measurement on tallow. We report it as such and will never use it otherwise.
    </p>

    <h3 class="sub-h">The asymmetry, which is the worst part</h3>
    <p class="measure">
      A lipase is a hydrolase of the carboxylic ester bond: for it to act, that bond must exist.
      A clean classification of the materials tallow is compared with follows from this.
    </p>
    <div class="tbl-wrap">
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Material</th><th scope="col">Chemical class</th><th scope="col">Ester bonds</th><th scope="col">Outcome</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {lipase.map((l) => (
            <tr class:list={[l.out === 'Exposed' && 'is-gap']}>
              <th scope="row">{l.mat}</th><td>{l.cls}</td><td>{l.bond}</td><td>{l.out}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      <DataTag kind="fonte" label="Classification by chemical class, and verification of alternative degradation routes for hydrocarbons and silicones, which are documented only in environmental matrices and not in skin commensals. Research track 6 v2, section 5.11.1." />
    </p>

    <Callout tone="honest" title="What this means exactly, with nothing softened">
      <p>
        This is not a difference of degree, it is a <strong>difference of chemical class</strong>.
        No process condition, no control of incoming acid value and no purification protocol can
        make a triglyceride immune to an enzyme that cuts ester bonds. It is a structural
        vulnerability, and it is asymmetric with respect to our commercially relevant
        comparators.
      </p>
      <p>
        Three qualifications define its boundaries, and all are needed so as neither to overstate
        nor to understate it. First: <strong>vegetable oils share the vulnerability</strong>, so
        the asymmetry does not exist on the ground of "tallow versus vegetable oils"; it exists
        only against the hydrocarbon and silicone block. Second:
        <strong>the comparators' immunity is not a benefit, it is the absence of a
        mechanism</strong>. Petrolatum and dimethicone do not generate free fatty acids, but they
        do not generate anything: they are inert. Third: <strong>the asymmetry concerns whether
        the mechanism exists, not the sign of its outcome</strong>. That we are exposed and they
        are not is a fact; that the exposure is harmful is precisely what has not been
        measured.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <h3 class="sub-h">How we phrase the claim, in full</h3>
    <blockquote class="claim">
      <p>
        Our tallow reaches the skin with a minimal load of free fatty acids and with a controlled
        oxidative state certified batch by batch. Like every lipid containing ester bonds, and
        therefore like every vegetable oil it is compared with, it is a substrate for microbial
        skin lipases: a share of it hydrolyses in situ. The available data on intact skin
        indicate that this process is markedly less extensive than what happens in an enclosed
        follicular environment. What is released is a mixture, and for its two main components
        the evidence runs in opposite directions: unfavourable for oleic acid, favourable for
        palmitic and stearic. <strong>The net effect on our material has never been measured, and
        we do not claim to know it.</strong> We are designing the experiment that would measure
        its composition, not only its rate. Compared with
        hydrocarbon and silicone comparators, which have no ester bonds and are therefore inert to
        this mechanism, our material is exposed and theirs is not: our thesis is not that we are
        as inert as they are, but that what we release is physiologically compatible and
        quantitatively contained, and that is what we intend to demonstrate.
      </p>
    </blockquote>
    <p class="note">
      It is longer and less sellable than an advertising line. It is also the only version that
      holds up in front of a competent formulator, and that is what the
      <a href={r.audit}>verification work</a> exists for.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section n="4" title="Physical state: the one genuinely favourable element, with its limits">
    <p>
      At skin surface temperature, around 32 °C, tallow is semisolid, with an estimated solid fat
      content of roughly 21%. It does not belong to the physical class of liquid oils but to that
      of petrolatum, whose melting range begins above skin temperature.
      <DataTag kind="stima" label="Solid fat content at 32 °C obtained by linear interpolation between 24.0% at 30 °C and 17.3% at 35 °C, pNMR measurements per AOCS Cd 16b-93. [DERIVED CALCULATION], not a direct measurement. The estimate is plausible but at the high end of the expected range, which is 15-25% at 32 °C: no publication reports an explicit numerical value for native tallow at that temperature, only curves in figures. Research track 4 v2 section 2.1, qualified by the multilingual library." />
    </p>
    <p class="measure">
      On this axis there is also a measured and citable datum rather than an estimate: tallow is
      <strong>fully melted only at 46 °C</strong>, whereas cocoa butter is already liquid between
      35 and 37 °C. At skin temperature the order is palm stearin, then beef tallow, then cocoa
      butter.
      <DataTag kind="fonte" label="Osborn HT, Akoh CC. Journal of Food Science 2002. DOI 10.1111/j.1365-2621.2002.tb08762.x. Comparison of melting profiles. It is the primary datum anchoring the thermal positioning of the material." />
    </p>
    <p class="ev-line"><Status kind="costruzione" lang={lang} /> <Finding kind="pro" lang={lang} /></p>
    <p class="measure">
      It matters because the most hostile prediction in the literature, that a high-oleic
      triglyceride does not reduce transepidermal water loss, was established on a
      <strong>liquid</strong> vegetable oil. If the variable separating the two arms of that
      comparison is physical state, the prediction is not automatically transferable to tallow.
    </p>

    <Callout tone="honest" title="What this argument does NOT demonstrate">
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>It does not demonstrate that tallow occludes.</strong> No measurement of
          transepidermal water loss on pure tallow applied as such was found in any of the
          research tracks.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Petrolatum's mechanism is not purely film-based.</strong> Petrolatum penetrates
          the stratum corneum and interacts with intercellular lipids. If its efficacy depends
          substantially on that interaction, sharing a physical state is irrelevant.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The two materials are semisolid for different reasons.</strong> At 32 °C tallow
          has already lost roughly four fifths of the solid fraction it had at room temperature.
          Solid fat content data for petrolatum by a comparable method could not be found, so the
          comparison stays qualitative.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The 79% liquid fraction is fully accessible.</strong> Even if the semisolid
          state slowed hydrolysis, that protection would cover about a fifth of the material, and
          slowing is not preventing.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        This is why the threshold is written <strong>now, before the measurement</strong>: if the
        solid fat content measured at 32 °C came out below 15%, the argument about a physical
        class akin to petrolatum falls, and the material has to be repositioned among the
        semi-liquid oils. The measurement costs a few hundred euros and is the first analytical
        spend of the plan, not the last.
      </p>
    </Callout>
    <p class="note">
      That the semisolid state slows hydrolysis <strong>is a hypothesis</strong>, built by analogy
      with what has been documented for digestive lipases in simulated digestion models, and not
      for skin lipases. Until it is tested experimentally it does not enter the positioning as a
      protective factor, and the criterion that would invalidate it is already written: it is in
      <a href={r.evidenza}>Open evidence</a>.
      <DataTag kind="bench" label="Analogy with Hart K, Lin S, Thilakarathna SH, Wright AJ. Emulsion droplet crystallinity attenuates early in vitro digestive lipolysis. Food Chem 2018;260:145-151. DOI 10.1016/j.foodchem.2018.03.142. The same study shows the difference vanishes by the end of digestion. No data on microbial skin lipases exists." />
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section n="5" title="Where we cannot compete">
    <p>
      A closed list, stated before you find it yourselves. None of these entries is negotiable
      with a smarter phrasing.
    </p>
    <ul class="cannot">
      {cannot.map((c) => (
        <li>
          <Finding kind="contro" lang={lang} />
          <h3>{c.t}</h3>
          <p>{c.d}</p>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <p class="note">
      <DataTag kind="fonte" label="Instrumental measurement of stickiness on calf tallow with a Texture Analyzer CT3, single-cycle compression, spherical nylon probe: Kowalska 2020, DOI 10.3390/biom10010115. The matrix is bovine and the measurement is instrumental rather than a panel judgment on an ovine matrix: both corrections strengthen the unfavourable datum." />
      <DataTag kind="fonte" label="Repair benchmark: physiological lipid systems in correct ratio assessed against fluticasone in a randomised trial on 121 patients. Research track 3 v4, section 5.5 and record sheet R04." />
    </p>

    <Callout tone="warn" title="The claim the category uses most, and why we do not">
      <p>
        "Tallow is similar to human sebum" is true for the triglyceride fraction only. Human
        sebum contains roughly 25% wax esters and 12-15% squalene, both absent from tallow, and
        its characteristic fatty acid is unique to the human species.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is not a phrasing to correct: it is a phrasing not to use. It appears nowhere else
        on this site, and you will find it in the <a href={r.audit}>claim taxonomy</a> among those
        defensible only in part.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </Section>

  <Section n="6" title="What follows">
    <p>
      Summing the five preceding sections: there is a solid chemical principle, there is a
      measurable and cheap parameter that governs it, there is a real complication that limits
      how long it holds, and there is no data at all on the cutaneous outcome of our material.
    </p>
    <p>
      The conclusion is not that tallow works. It is that <strong>nobody knows whether it works,
      and nobody is measuring what is inside</strong>. Under those conditions the only defensible
      activity is measurement, and the only product that can be sold without lying is the
      document that reports it.
    </p>
    <div class="closing">
      <p>The tallow is the carrier. The document is the product.</p>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      What that document contains, field by field, is described in <a href={r.amsa}>AMSA</a>.
      Where the raw material comes from, and where its traceability breaks, is in
      <a href={r.filiera}>Supply chain</a>. What a formulator can tell their own customer without
      exposure is in <a href={r.normativa}>Regulation</a>.
    </p>
  </Section>

